{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 46, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III was born in 1140 and became the Count of Tripoli at the age of 12 after the death of his father.\n\n*Early Life and Reign*\nRaymond III was born in 1140 to Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His father was the Count of Tripoli and his mother was the daughter of Baldwin II of Jerusalem. Raymond III became the Count of Tripoli at the age of 12 after the death of his father in 1152. His mother Hodierna acted as regent for him until he came of age.\n\nRaymond III was a key player in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the 12th century. He was a cousin of King Amalric I of Jerusalem and played a crucial role in the kingdom's conflicts with the Muslim states of the region. In 1164 Raymond III was captured by the forces of Nur ad-Din while leading a campaign against the city of Homs. He was imprisoned for several years before being released in 1173.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin*\nIn 1187 Raymond III led a contingent of troops from Tripoli to join the army of King Guy of Lusignan at the Battle of Hattin. The battle was a disaster for the Kingdom of Jerusalem and Raymond III was forced to flee the battlefield. The defeat at Hattin marked the beginning of the end of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and Raymond III played a key role in the subsequent events.\n\n*Later Life and Death*\nAfter the Battle of Hattin Raymond III returned to Tripoli and began to consolidate his power in the city. He died in 1187 and was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV. Raymond III was remembered as a skilled military leader and a key player in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the 12th century.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.004134069196879864}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "